<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/9th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Bathys Ryax</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">872 or 878</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Bathys Ryax was fought in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> between the Byzantine army of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-i">Basil I</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paulicians">Paulicians</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divrigi">Tephrike</a>, probably in 872 or 878. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> surprised the Paulician army as it withdrew from a raid into imperial territory, and its leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chrysocheir">Chrysocheir</a> was killed. The defeat broke Paulician power, and Tephrike fell to the Byzantines soon afterwards.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-bathys-ryax.jpg" alt="Battle of Bathys Ryax" width="320" height="168" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cplakidas · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Asia_Minor_ca_842_AD.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paulicians">Paulicians</a> were a Christian sect whose origins and beliefs are obscure: Byzantine sources portray them as dualists, Armenian sources as adoptionists. Fiercely iconoclastic, they rejected the authority of the official Church. Persecuted from as early as 813, they suffered worse after the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-iconoclasm">Byzantine Iconoclasm</a> in 843, when orders went out to kill all who would not recant; according to the chroniclers up to 100,000 were massacred. The survivors fled to the Arab emirates of the Thughur, and with the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umar-al-aqta">Umar al-Aqta</a>, emir of Melitene, their leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karbeas">Karbeas</a> founded a principality at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divrigi">Tephrike</a>.</p><p>Umar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karbeas">Karbeas</a> both died in 863, but under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chrysocheir">Chrysocheir</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paulicians">Paulicians</a> raided deep into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, reaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> and sacking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> in 869/870. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-i">Basil I</a> the Macedonian campaigned against them in the spring of 871, only to be defeated and barely escape.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chrysocheir">Chrysocheir</a> then raided as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ancyra</a>. Basil sent his relative Christopher, the Domestic of the Schools, after him, and as the campaigning season ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paulicians">Paulicians</a> withdrew towards the pass of Bathys Ryax, the Deep Stream, west of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sivas">Sivas</a>. Christopher sent the strategoi of the themes of Armeniakon and Charsianon ahead with four to five thousand men to follow them and report. Reaching the pass at nightfall, they found the Paulicians camped in the valley below a wooded hill, Zogoloenos, which hid them.</p><p>After a dispute between the two corps over which was the braver, the generals attacked against their orders. At dawn a picked force of 600 men charged while the rest sounded trumpets and drums to suggest that Christopher's whole army was arriving. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paulicians">Paulicians</a> panicked, fled into the main Byzantine army and were pursued for 50 km. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chrysocheir">Chrysocheir</a> was brought to bay at Konstantinou Bounos, wounded by Poulades, a former captive of the Paulicians, and beheaded; his head was sent to Basil.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The defeat ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paulicians">Paulicians</a> as a military power. Basil followed it with campaigns against their strongholds and the Arab emirates; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divrigi">Tephrike</a> was razed, and the remaining Paulicians were resettled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, with a large contingent sent to Southern Italy under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikephoros-phokas-the-elder">Nikephoros Phokas the Elder</a>. Because the Byzantine sources conflict, the order of events is uncertain: Paul Lemerle, followed by Mark Whittow and Warren Treadgold, put the battle in 872 and the subjugation of Tephrike later, while Nina Garsoïan and John Haldon place both in 878.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Bathys Ryax” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
